    part writePathCensusRowsHaveARefusalBehindThemImplementGateR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "d2a6b899-4e5b-4f03-a2ea-cee5cc255d06"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "4b7467c"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-09-13"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeFable5"; }
    verification writePathCensusRowsHaveARefusalBehindThemReviewGate : Test { :>> id = "80fd1b13-14ad-4e48-9291-cec7c04e9c69"; :>> title = "Sprint 701 review gate — three passes per D0172"; :>> createdAt = "2026-09-13"; :>> createdBy = "claudeFable5"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::inspect; :>> procedureText = "Three passes (D0172). Correctness: the registry's tests read src/write.rs and src/main.rs as text, the idiom write.rs already uses for its lock-scope control; a WriteError token must be both declared as a variant and returned somewhere in write.rs, a bare token must be a fn in main.rs and its check literal must appear as a ledger_refused argument; the main.rs parser takes only single-line three-argument calls, so a call whose verb is a variable is matched on its check alone - accept, reject and judge-set share verdict_channel_refusal by design. The pass found what the DoD had not named: keel reject (cmd_reject, main.rs:5087) refuses through the same channel and had ledgered a fire, and the census had no reject row - a refusal with no row, issue449's mirror; fixed here with three entries and three rows, and the census test is what would have caught it. Honesty: the registry is a second enumeration beside the census table, and the census test is what keeps the two equal; a refusal that ledgers under a variable verb with a check no entry names is reported by the main.rs test, not silently counted. Scope: no census row was removed; the three dissolved rows stay as history and are excluded from the live set by their state text, the same field the census renders."; }
